	Well, sorta.  Let me explain...

>1) How do you convert an altar?

	Drag a fresh corpse over to the altar and #offer it.  If you
haven't actively annoyed your god, you will try to convert the altar.
Your chance of failure (from v3.0j) is 5/your exp. level+8, so a 2nd
level character has a 50% chance of failure, while a 17th level
character has only a 20% chance.

	Each time you fail, your god gets slightly miffed (subtracts
some small amount from your alignment).

>2) Can you pray only at your god's altar, or any co-aligned altar?

	You can pray anywhere, even when you're not standing on an
altar.  If you pray while standing on a non-co-aligned altar, however,
your god will get rather angry.  (subtracts some large amount from
your alignment)

>3) What are the possible effects of #offering?

	You can improve your alignment ("the gods seem mollified."),
improve your luck ("you notice a four-leaf clover near your feet."),
or even be granted a gift ("an object appears near your feet!")  I'm
certain there are more possible effects, but those are all that I can
remember right now.

>4) Is there a way to figure out exactly what sort of monster corpse is
>going to make the Deity happy and what's going to be ignored (based on
>time, your level, dungeon level, and monster level)?

	The bigger, the better.  The thing that really matters is
the level of the monster being sacrificed in relation to the
character's level.  A first level character might be recognized for
slaying a hobgoblin; a 30th level character would have to offer a
dragon corpse to get any recognition at all.  (Too bad demons don't
leave corpses.)

